// `[using arithmetic fallback(<axiom-or-hole>)]`: a fallback may cite an AXIOM (a
// hole is an axiom-kind statement), not only a theorem. The goal here is an
// ORDER goal `less_than(a, succ(a))`: `[using arithmetic]` DECIDES it, but the
// order certifier needs `lessThanIntro` to synthesize the witness — and it is
// deliberately NOT in scope, so the certifier declines. The hole
// `hasPredecessorHole` then discharges the step by fiat. The proof rests on a
// hole: rejected under strict `bpa check`, accepted under `--draft`.
//
// (A decide-but-can't-certify goal is used on purpose so the fallback stays
// genuinely needed — a goal the certifier CAN prove would make the fallback
// redundant, which is itself a hard error.)
sort Int
const ZERO: Int
func succ(n: Int) => Int
func add(a: Int, b: Int) => Int
pred less_than(a: Int, b: Int)

hole boundHole: forall a: Int; less_than(a, succ(a))

theorem bound: forall a: Int; less_than(a, succ(a))
proof
  @conclusion |
    forall a: Int; less_than(a, succ(a))
    [using arithmetic fallback(boundHole)]
qed
